OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Maximal-multipoint-Seshadri-constants-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026", "title": "Maximal multipoint Seshadri constants in positive characteristic", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Maximal-multipoint-Seshadri-constants-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/seshadri-positive-characteristic.pdf", "family": "039", "family_title": "Nagata’s conjecture and maximal Seshadri constants", "abstract_html": "Let <i>L</i> be an ample line bundle on a smooth integral projective variety <i>X</i> over an algebraically closed field of positive characteristic, with \\(\\dim X=n\\ge3\\). We prove that there is a threshold \\(r_0=r_0(X,L)\\) such that for every integer \\(r\\ge r_0\\), the ordinary multipoint Seshadri constant at the geometric generic tuple of <i>r</i> points equals the volume bound \\((L^n/r)^{1/n}\\). The same conclusion holds in dimension two. This establishes the positive-characteristic form of the qualitative Nagata–Biran–Szemberg assertion at geometric generic tuples.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Theorem 1.1 of the paper, including the surface conclusion in Remark 6.3.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Theorem 1.1 of the paper, including the surface conclusion in Remark 6.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be an algebraically closed field of characteristic \\(p&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X/k\\) be a smooth integral projective variety of dimension \\(n\\geq 3\\), and let \\(L\\) be an ample line bundle.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(r\\geq 1\\), let\n  \\[\n  U_r=\\{(x_1,\\ldots,x_r)\\in X^r:x_i\\neq x_j\\text{ for }i\\neq j\\}\n  \\]\n  be the open configuration space of ordered distinct points. Put\n  \\[\n  K_r=\\overline{k(U_r)},\n  \\]\n  and let \\(p_1,\\ldots,p_r\\in X(K_r)\\) be the points specified by its geometric generic point.</p><p class=\"li\">Define the ordinary, curve-defined multipoint Seshadri constant by\n  \\[\n  \\varepsilon_r(X,L)\n  =\n  \\inf_C\n  \\frac{L_{K_r}\\cdot C}\n       {\\sum_{i=1}^r\\operatorname{mult}_{p_i}C},\n  \\]\n  where \\(C\\) ranges over integral curves in \\(X_{K_r}\\) meeting at least one \\(p_i\\). Multiplicity at a point outside \\(C\\) is zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an integer \\(r_0=r_0(X,L)\\) such that\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_r(X,L)\n=\n\\left(\\frac{L^n}{r}\\right)^{1/n}\n\\qquad\\text{for every integer }r\\geq r_0.\n\\]\nThe same conclusion holds when \\(n=2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(r_0\\) as in Proposition 5 and fix \\(r\\geq r_0\\). Put\n\\[\nV=L^n,\\qquad w=(V/r)^{1/n}.\n\\]\nWork over \\(K_r\\) at the geometric generic tuple \\(p_1,\\ldots,p_r\\).</p><p>For every fixed real number \\(0&lt;\\theta&lt;w\\) and all sufficiently large integers \\(N\\), Proposition 5 gives separation of jets through order\n\\[\nm=\\lfloor N\\theta\\rfloor\n\\]\nby\n\\[\nH^0(X_{K_r},L_{K_r}^{\\otimes N})\n\\]\nat this fixed tuple.</p><p>For large \\(N\\), one has \\(m\\geq1\\), so Proposition 6 yields\n\\[\n\\frac{L_{K_r}\\cdot C}\n     {\\sum_i\\operatorname{mult}_{p_i}C}\n\\geq\n\\frac{\\lfloor N\\theta\\rfloor}{N}\n\\]\nfor every integral curve in the defining infimum. Letting \\(N\\) tend to infinity gives\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_r(X,L)\\geq\\theta.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\theta&lt;w\\) was arbitrary,\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_r(X,L)\\geq w.\n\\]</p><p>Intersection numbers are unchanged by extension of the algebraically closed ground field, so\n\\[\n(L_{K_r})^n=V.\n\\]\nProposition 7 supplies the reverse inequality, proving\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_r(X,L)=\\left(\\frac{L^n}{r}\\right)^{1/n}.\n\\]</p><p>For the surface conclusion, as stated in Remark 6.3 of the paper, Propositions 3, 4, and 5 all allow \\(n=2\\); the reshaping is then performed just once, with no unchanged coordinates. Propositions 6 and 7 also apply in dimension two. The same proof therefore establishes the asserted equality for \\(n=2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Leading exponents, openness of jet separation, and backward transfer of jets; Section 2 of the paper.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Leading exponents, openness of jet separation, and backward transfer of jets; Section 2 of the paper.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Here \\(k\\) is algebraically closed, with arbitrary characteristic; the positive-characteristic restriction of Theorem 1 is not required.</p><p class=\"li\">For a smooth \\(d\\)-dimensional \\(k\\)-variety \\(Y\\), a line bundle \\(A\\), a point \\(y\\in Y(k)\\), and an integer \\(m\\geq 0\\), write\n  \\[\n  J_y^m(A)=A_y/\\mathfrak m_y^{m+1}A_y.\n  \\]\n  A finite-dimensional space \\(W\\subset H^0(Y,A)\\) separates jets through order \\(m\\) at \\(y_1,\\ldots,y_r\\) if\n  \\[\n  W\\longrightarrow\\bigoplus_{i=1}^rJ_{y_i}^m(A)\n  \\]\n  is surjective.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(T^d=(\\mathbb G_{m,k})^d\\). For a finite set \\(S\\subset\\mathbb Z^d\\), put\n  \\[\n  M(S)=\\operatorname{span}_k\\{x^\\alpha:\\alpha\\in S\\}\n  \\subset k[x_1^{\\pm1},\\ldots,x_d^{\\pm1}].\n  \\]\n  Auxiliary existence statements about torus points refer to \\(k\\)-points.</p><p class=\"li\">As in Definition 2.1 of the paper, a family of finite sets \\((S_N)_{N\\geq1}\\) in \\(\\mathbb Z^d\\) is graded if\n  \\[\n  S_N+S_{N'}\\subset S_{N+N'}\n  \\qquad(N,N'\\geq1).\n  \\]\n  A transformation \\((S_N)\\mapsto(T_N)\\) has backward jet transfer if, for every \\(N,r\\geq1\\) and \\(m\\geq0\\), jet separation through order \\(m\\) by \\(M(T_N)\\) at some \\(r\\) distinct torus points implies the same assertion for \\(M(S_N)\\), possibly at a different tuple.</p><p class=\"li\">A monomial order on \\(\\mathbb Z_{\\geq0}^h\\) is an addition-compatible total well-order. For a nonzero formal series \\(f\\in k[[z_1,\\ldots,z_h]]\\), let \\(\\nu(f)\\) be its least exponent for the chosen order.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. The locus of ordered distinct tuples at which a fixed finite-dimensional space \\(W\\subset H^0(Y,A)\\) separates jets through order \\(m\\) is open. Jet evaluation is compatible with field extension.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For every finite-dimensional subspace \\(W\\subset k[[z_1,\\ldots,z_h]]\\), its set of least exponents has cardinality \\(\\dim_kW\\). If finite-dimensional spaces \\(W_{N,\\gamma}\\), with \\(\\gamma\\in\\mathbb Z^{d-h}\\) and only finitely many nonzero spaces in each degree, satisfy\n   \\[\n   W_{N,\\gamma}W_{N',\\gamma'}\n   \\subset W_{N+N',\\gamma+\\gamma'},\n   \\]\n   then\n   \\[\n   E_N=\n   \\{(\\nu(f),\\gamma):0\\neq f\\in W_{N,\\gamma}\\}\n   \\]\n   is a graded family, with\n   \\[\n   \\#E_N=\\sum_\\gamma\\dim_kW_{N,\\gamma}.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Suppose \\(1\\leq h\\leq d\\), \\(Y\\) is smooth integral of dimension \\(h\\), \\(y\\in Y(k)\\) has formal coordinates \\(z_1,\\ldots,z_h\\), and \\(f_1,\\ldots,f_q\\) are nonzero regular functions on a common neighborhood of \\(y\\). Let \\(\\gamma_j\\in\\mathbb Z^{d-h}\\) and \\(e_j=\\nu(f_j)\\). Assume that the order is either lexicographic, or that \\(h=2\\) and it compares\n   \\[\n   (\\alpha+t\\beta,\\beta)\n   \\]\n   lexicographically for a fixed real \\(t&gt;0\\).</p><p>   If the span of \\(Z^{e_j}u^{\\gamma_j}\\) separates jets through order \\(m\\) at \\(r\\) distinct points of \\(T^h\\times T^{d-h}\\), then the span of \\(f_ju^{\\gamma_j}\\) separates the same jets at \\(r\\) distinct points in the product of that neighborhood with \\(T^{d-h}\\). The points may be chosen in any prescribed dense open subset of this product. The statement also holds for sections written in a local frame of a line bundle.</p><p>Thus least exponents, with unchanged variables recorded as labels, preserve dimension and gradedness and transfer jet separation back to the original functions or sections.</p>", "proof": "<p>For openness, the jet spaces are the fibers of the locally free sheaf of principal parts of \\(A\\), of rank \\(\\binom{d+m}{d}\\), and surjectivity is a rank condition. The description of principal parts by the \\(m\\)-th infinitesimal neighborhood of the diagonal also shows compatibility with field extension. Local coordinates for a smooth variety identify its fibers with truncated polynomial rings. Local freeness follows as well from the filtration whose graded pieces are\n\\[\nA\\otimes\\operatorname{Sym}^j\\Omega_Y^1,\n\\qquad 0\\leq j\\leq m.\n\\]</p><p>Lemma 1.1 proves the cardinality and gradedness statements.</p><p>For the transfer assertion, Lemma 1.2 realizes the finitely many chosen least terms by a single positive integral weight vector. Lemma 1.3 then transfers the jet-separation statement to the original functions or sections. For finite-dimensional spaces, choose bases with distinct least exponents as in Lemma 1.1. When unchanged variables are present, their exponents are retained as labels.</p><p>The auxiliary weights may depend on the degree; the monomial order defining the graded family remains fixed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Lemma 2.2 of the paper.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Lemma 2.2 of the paper.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be algebraically closed, of arbitrary characteristic.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a monomial order on \\(\\mathbb Z_{\\geq0}^h\\), and let \\(\\nu(f)\\) denote the least exponent of a nonzero series \\(f\\in k[[z_1,\\ldots,z_h]]\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For a finite-dimensional subspace \\(W\\subset k[[z_1,\\ldots,z_h]]\\), the set\n\\[\n\\{\\nu(f):0\\neq f\\in W\\}\n\\]\nhas cardinality \\(\\dim_kW\\).</p><p>More generally, suppose finite-dimensional spaces \\(W_{N,\\gamma}\\), with only finitely many nonzero spaces in each degree, are indexed by \\(N\\geq1\\) and \\(\\gamma\\in\\mathbb Z^{d-h}\\), and satisfy\n\\[\nW_{N,\\gamma}W_{N',\\gamma'}\n\\subset W_{N+N',\\gamma+\\gamma'}.\n\\]\nThen recording the pairs \\((\\nu(f),\\gamma)\\) for \\(0\\neq f\\in W_{N,\\gamma}\\) gives a graded family, with cardinality\n\\[\n\\sum_\\gamma\\dim_kW_{N,\\gamma}\n\\]\nin degree \\(N\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The least exponent is multiplicative:\n\\[\n\\nu(fg)=\\nu(f)+\\nu(g).\n\\]\nIndeed, the product of the two least terms is the unique least term in the product.</p><p>Choose the smallest exponent occurring as \\(\\nu(f)\\) for a nonzero member of \\(W\\), and choose a member whose coefficient there is \\(1\\). The kernel of that coefficient functional has codimension one. Repeating in this kernel gives a basis with distinct least exponents. Every nonzero linear combination has as its least exponent the smallest least exponent among the basis vectors with nonzero coefficients.</p><p>This proves the first assertion; the second follows label by label and from multiplicativity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Lemma 2.3 of the paper.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Lemma 2.3 of the paper.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f_1,\\ldots,f_q\\) be nonzero formal series in \\(k[[z_1,\\ldots,z_h]]\\), and let \\(e_j=\\nu(f_j)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume either:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. the order is lexicographic; or</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. \\(h=2\\) and, for a fixed real \\(t&gt;0\\), the order compares \\((\\alpha+t\\beta,\\beta)\\) lexicographically.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a vector \\(\\lambda\\in\\mathbb Z_{&gt;0}^h\\) for which \\(e_j\\) is the unique exponent of least \\(\\lambda\\)-weight in \\(f_j\\), simultaneously for all \\(j\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>In the lexicographic case set \\(\\lambda_h=1\\), and successively choose\n\\[\n\\lambda_i&gt;\n\\max_j\\sum_{a&gt;i}\\lambda_a(e_j)_a\n\\qquad(i=h-1,\\ldots,1).\n\\]\nIf an exponent is lexicographically greater than \\(e_j\\), its gain at the first differing coordinate outweighs every possible loss in the later coordinates.</p><p>For the second case, the indicated order is a monomial order because every bounded weight range contains only finitely many nonnegative integral exponents. Choose a real bound larger than the weights of all the \\(e_j\\). Among the finitely many exponents below that bound, replacing \\(t\\) by a sufficiently close rational \\(t'&gt;t\\) preserves all strict comparisons with the \\(e_j\\) and resolves equal-weight comparisons by increasing \\(\\beta\\).</p><p>Exponents above the bound cannot interfere: their weights only increase, while the new weights of the \\(e_j\\) stay below the bound. Clearing the denominator of \\((1,t')\\) gives the required positive integral weights.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Lemma 2.4 of the paper:", "statement": "<p>**Lemma 2.4 of the paper: Formal jet transfer.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be algebraically closed, of arbitrary characteristic.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(1\\leq h\\leq d\\), let \\(Y\\) be a smooth integral \\(h\\)-dimensional \\(k\\)-variety, and choose \\(y\\in Y(k)\\) with formal coordinates \\(z_1,\\ldots,z_h\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f_1,\\ldots,f_q\\) be regular functions on a common neighborhood of \\(y\\), and let \\(\\gamma_j\\in\\mathbb Z^{d-h}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose the expansion of \\(f_j\\) has a term \\(c_jz^{e_j}\\), with \\(c_j\\neq0\\), which is uniquely lowest for one common weight vector \\(\\lambda\\in\\mathbb Z_{&gt;0}^h\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(r\\geq1\\) and \\(m\\geq0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If the span of the monomials \\(Z^{e_j}u^{\\gamma_j}\\) separates jets through order \\(m\\) at \\(r\\) distinct points of \\(T^h\\times T^{d-h}\\), then the span of \\(f_ju^{\\gamma_j}\\) separates the same jets at \\(r\\) distinct points in the product of that neighborhood with \\(T^{d-h}\\).</p><p>The points may be chosen in any prescribed dense open subset of this product. The statement also holds for sections written in a local frame of a line bundle.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\((Q_i,U_i)\\), \\(1\\leq i\\leq r\\), be a tuple at which the monomials separate the stated jets. Shrink the neighborhood of \\(y\\) to an affine one, denoted \\(Y^\\circ\\), on which all the functions are defined. Put\n\\[\nR=k[[s]],\\qquad F=k((s)),\\qquad\nB=R[\\delta_1,\\ldots,\\delta_d]/(\\delta_1,\\ldots,\\delta_d)^{m+1},\n\\]\nand write \\(\\delta=(\\delta',\\delta'')\\) according to the two factors. For each \\(i\\), substitute\n\\[\nz_a=s^{\\lambda_a}(Q_{i,a}+\\delta'_a),\n\\qquad\nu_b=U_{i,b}+\\delta''_b.\n\\]\nThe first substitution is defined on the completed local ring, since \\(B\\) is \\(s\\)-adically complete and every \\(\\lambda_a\\) is positive. The second is defined on Laurent polynomials, since each \\(U_{i,b}\\) is nonzero. In particular, these substitutions define ring maps from the coordinate ring of \\(Y^\\circ\\times T^{d-h}\\) to \\(B\\).</p><p>Use the classes of \\(\\delta^\\alpha\\), \\(|\\alpha|\\leq m\\), as an \\(R\\)-basis of \\(B\\). The images of \\(f_ju^{\\gamma_j}\\) under all \\(r\\) substitutions give the \\(j\\)-th column of a matrix with\n\\[\nr\\binom{d+m}{d}\n\\]\nrows. Divide this column by \\(c_js^{\\lambda\\cdot e_j}\\). These normalized columns still belong to \\(B^{\\oplus r}\\), and their reductions modulo \\(s\\) are precisely the jets of \\(Z^{e_j}u^{\\gamma_j}\\) at \\((Q_i,U_i)\\) in translated coordinates. The assumed surjectivity supplies a maximal minor nonzero modulo \\(s\\). The original columns therefore span \\(B[1/s]^{\\oplus r}\\) over \\(F\\).</p><p>We now interpret this rank statement as jet separation on a variety. Setting \\(\\delta=0\\) in the substitutions gives actual \\(F\\)-points \\(P_i\\) of \\(Y^\\circ\\times T^{d-h}\\). After inverting \\(s\\), the corresponding ring map factors through the order-\\(m\\) jet algebra of \\((Y^\\circ\\times T^{d-h})_F\\) at \\(P_i\\): elements nonvanishing at \\(P_i\\) map to units, and its maximal ideal maps into \\((\\delta)\\).</p><p>Both this jet algebra and \\(B[1/s]\\) have dimension \\(\\binom{d+m}{d}\\) over \\(F\\). Surjectivity onto the direct sum of the latter spaces forces each induced map from a jet algebra to be an isomorphism. It follows that the original functions separate the order-\\(m\\) jets at the \\(P_i\\) themselves. These points are distinct, since two repeated centers would make two target blocks factor through one jet algebra, contradicting surjectivity.</p><p>Thus the open jet-separation locus has an \\(F\\)-point and is nonempty. The configuration space is irreducible, so this locus meets the configuration space of any prescribed dense open subset. A nonempty open subset of a variety over the algebraically closed field \\(k\\) has a \\(k\\)-point. This proves the assertion. A local frame reduces the line-bundle version to the one just proved.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**The elementary transformations of Section 2.3 of the paper.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**The elementary transformations of Section 2.3 of the paper.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be algebraically closed, of arbitrary characteristic.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((S_N)_{N\\geq1}\\) be a graded family of finite subsets of \\(\\mathbb Z^d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the definitions of \\(M(S)\\) and backward jet transfer from Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. For \\(A\\in\\operatorname{GL}_d(\\mathbb Z)\\), the transformation\n   \\[\n   S_N\\longmapsto AS_N\n   \\]\n   preserves cardinalities and gradedness and has backward jet transfer.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Select one coordinate, written \\(z\\). For each degree \\(N\\) and each label \\(\\gamma\\in\\mathbb Z^{d-1}\\), form the row space\n   \\[\n   W_{N,\\gamma}\n   =\n   \\operatorname{span}_k\\{z^a:(a,\\gamma)\\in S_N\\}.\n   \\]\n   Replace this row by the set of orders at \\(z=1\\) of its nonzero elements, retaining the label \\(\\gamma\\). This coordinate compression preserves cardinalities and gradedness and has backward jet transfer.</p><p>   If the original row lies in a real interval of length \\(b\\), every new exponent lies in \\([0,b]\\).</p><p>Consequently, for a family bounded by dilates of a polygon, replacing each slice parallel to the compressed coordinate by an interval of the same length starting at zero gives a region whose dilates contain the compressed family. This applies to real slice endpoints and to every dilation.</p>", "proof": "<p>The two transformations have the asserted properties by Lemmas 2.1 and 2.2.</p><p>For the geometric consequence, apply the interval bound of Lemma 2.2 to every slice of the bounding polygon parallel to the compressed coordinate. Replacing that slice by an interval of the same length starting at zero bounds the compressed row. The same argument applies to real endpoints and to every dilation of the polygon.</p><p>In characteristic \\(p\\), a compressed row need not contain all intermediate integers: for example,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{span}_k\\{1,z^p\\}\n\\]\nhas orders \\(0\\) and \\(p\\) at \\(1\\). Only the interval bound is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Lemma 2.5 of the paper.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Lemma 2.5 of the paper.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((S_N)\\) be a graded family of finite subsets of \\(\\mathbb Z^d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\in\\operatorname{GL}_d(\\mathbb Z)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The transformation \\(S_N\\mapsto AS_N\\) preserves cardinalities and gradedness and has backward jet transfer.</p>", "proof": "<p>An invertible integral linear map preserves cardinalities and addition. Its action on characters is induced by a torus automorphism, which identifies the corresponding jet evaluations.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Lemma 2.6 of the paper:", "statement": "<p>**Lemma 2.6 of the paper: Coordinate compression.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be algebraically closed, of arbitrary characteristic.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((S_N)\\) be a graded family in \\(\\mathbb Z^d\\), and select one coordinate, written \\(z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For each degree \\(N\\) and each label \\(\\gamma\\in\\mathbb Z^{d-1}\\), form\n  \\[\n  W_{N,\\gamma}\n  =\n  \\operatorname{span}_k\\{z^a:(a,\\gamma)\\in S_N\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Replace each row by the set of orders at \\(z=1\\) of nonzero elements of \\(W_{N,\\gamma}\\), retaining the label \\(\\gamma\\). The resulting family preserves cardinalities and gradedness and has backward jet transfer.</p><p>If the original row lies in a real interval of length \\(b\\), every new exponent lies in \\([0,b]\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Expand the row spaces in the parameter \\(z-1\\). The least-exponent statement of Proposition 1 proves the cardinality and gradedness assertions, because multiplication adds both degrees and labels. Its formal jet-transfer statement, with \\(Y=\\mathbb G_m\\), \\(y=1\\), and weight \\(1\\), proves backward jet transfer.</p><p>For a nonempty row let \\(a_-\\) and \\(a_+\\) be its smallest and largest exponents. Multiplication by \\(z^{-a_-}\\) preserves orders at \\(1\\) and turns each member into a polynomial of degree at most\n\\[\na_+-a_-\\leq b.\n\\]\nIts order of vanishing is at most its degree, proving the bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Proposition 3.1 of the paper:", "statement": "<p>**Proposition 3.1 of the paper: An initial simplex from a flag.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be algebraically closed, of arbitrary characteristic.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X/k\\) be a smooth integral projective variety of dimension \\(n\\geq2\\), and let \\(L\\) be ample.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(V=L^n\\), and choose an integer \\(D\\geq1\\) such that \\(DL\\) is very ample.</p><p class=\"li\">For positive real numbers \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_d\\), write\n  \\[\n  \\Delta(a_1,\\ldots,a_d)\n  =\n  \\left\\{\n  x\\in\\mathbb R_{\\geq0}^d:\n  \\sum_{i=1}^d\\frac{x_i}{a_i}\\leq1\n  \\right\\}.\n  \\]\n  Its volume is \\(\\prod_i a_i/d!\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use additive notation \\(NL\\) for \\(L^{\\otimes N}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a graded family of finite sets \\(S_N\\subset\\mathbb Z_{\\geq0}^n\\) such that\n\\[\nS_N\\subset NP_0,\n\\qquad\nP_0=\\Delta(1/D,\\ldots,1/D,D^{n-1}V),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\#S_N=h^0(X,NL)=\\frac{V}{n!}N^n+o(N^n).\n\\]</p><p>For every \\(N,r\\geq1\\) and \\(m\\geq0\\), if \\(M(S_N)\\) separates jets through order \\(m\\) at some \\(r\\) distinct torus points, then \\(H^0(X,NL)\\) separates the same jets at some \\(r\\) distinct points of \\(X\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Bertini’s theorem for hyperplane sections, valid in every characteristic for a projective embedding over an algebraically closed field, gives sections\n\\[\n\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_{n-1}\\in H^0(X,DL)\n\\]\nwhose successive zero loci form a smooth integral flag\n\\[\nX=X_0\\supset X_1\\supset\\cdots\\supset X_{n-1}=C_0.\n\\]\nAt each step the variety being cut has dimension at least two.</p><p>Choose \\(x\\in C_0(k)\\) and a local frame \\(e\\) of \\(L\\) near \\(x\\). The functions\n\\[\nz_i=\\sigma_i/e^D,\\qquad i&lt;n,\n\\]\nform part of a regular system of parameters at \\(x\\); complete them by \\(z_n\\). These parameters identify\n\\[\n\\widehat{\\mathcal O}_{X,x}\\simeq k[[z_1,\\ldots,z_n]].\n\\]</p><p>Expand every section of \\(NL\\) using the frame \\(e^N\\), and let \\(S_N\\) be the set of lexicographically least exponents of the nonzero expansions. The expansion map is injective: a section with zero germ is zero by integrality, and the local ring injects into its completion. Proposition 1 therefore gives\n\\[\n\\#S_N=h^0(X,NL)\n\\]\nand the graded property. The Hilbert polynomial of the ample line bundle gives\n\\[\nh^0(X,NL)=\\frac{V}{n!}N^n+o(N^n).\n\\]\nA basis with distinct least exponents and the lexicographic formal-transfer assertion of Proposition 1 prove the jet-transfer assertion.</p><p>It remains to prove the simplex bound. Let\n\\[\n\\alpha=(\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_n)\\in S_N\n\\]\ncome from a nonzero section \\(s\\). We successively divide by the equations of the flag and restrict to the next member. The lexicographic order first measures vanishing along an entire divisor, not just at the chosen point.</p><p>Suppose we have reached a nonzero section on \\(X_{i-1}\\) with least exponent \\((\\alpha_i,\\ldots,\\alpha_n)\\) in its formal expansion at \\(x\\). If \\(\\alpha_i&gt;0\\), restriction of this section to \\(X_i\\) has zero formal expansion. Injectivity into the completed local ring, followed by integrality of \\(X_i\\), shows that this restriction is identically zero. The exact sequence for the effective Cartier divisor \\(X_i\\subset X_{i-1}\\) therefore allows division by \\(\\sigma_i|_{X_{i-1}}\\).</p><p>Repeating this argument \\(\\alpha_i\\) times, and then restricting to \\(X_i\\), gives a nonzero section whose least exponent is \\((\\alpha_{i+1},\\ldots,\\alpha_n)\\). Nonvanishing follows from the nonzero coefficient of the original least term. Each division subtracts \\(DL\\) from the line bundle.</p><p>After these steps, the resulting nonzero section on \\(C_0\\) belongs to\n\\[\n\\left(N-D\\sum_{i&lt;n}\\alpha_i\\right)L|_{C_0}\n\\]\nand has order exactly \\(\\alpha_n\\) at \\(x\\). Since\n\\[\nL\\cdot C_0=D^{n-1}V,\n\\]\ncomparison with the degree of its zero divisor gives\n\\[\n\\alpha_n\n\\leq\n\\left(N-D\\sum_{i&lt;n}\\alpha_i\\right)D^{n-1}V.\n\\]\nEquivalently,\n\\[\nD\\sum_{i&lt;n}\\alpha_i+\\frac{\\alpha_n}{D^{n-1}V}\\leq N,\n\\]\nwhich is the required simplex bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Proposition 4.1 of the paper:", "statement": "<p>**Proposition 4.1 of the paper: Two-intercept reshaping.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be algebraically closed, of arbitrary characteristic.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\geq2\\), let \\(A,B,a_3,\\ldots,a_n&gt;0\\), and let \\((S_N)_{N\\geq1}\\) be a graded family of finite subsets of \\(\\mathbb Z^n\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  S_N\\subset N\\Delta(A,B,a_3,\\ldots,a_n).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  H=AB,\\qquad d=\\max\\{3/A,2/B\\},\n  \\]\n  and define\n  \\[\n  W=dH=\\max\\{2A,3B\\},\\qquad\n  c=\\frac1d,\\qquad\n  T=d^2H=\\max\\{9B/A,4A/B\\}\\geq6.\n  \\]\n  In particular, \\(cW=H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(w&gt;0\\) such that \\(dw&lt;1/16\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(n=2\\), the lists \\(a_3,\\ldots,a_n\\) are empty.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a graded family \\((S'_N)_{N\\geq1}\\) such that\n\\[\nS'_N\\subset N\\Delta(w,H/w,a_3,\\ldots,a_n),\n\\qquad\n\\#S'_N=\\#S_N\n\\qquad(N\\geq1).\n\\]\nThe transformation from \\(S_N\\) to \\(S'_N\\) has backward jet transfer in every degree.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(t\\) and \\(u\\) from Lemma 4.3; these choices will be used in every degree. Keep coordinates \\(3,\\ldots,n\\) unchanged. For each degree \\(N\\) and each label\n\\[\n\\gamma=(\\gamma_3,\\ldots,\\gamma_n)\n\\]\noccurring in \\(S_N\\), put\n\\[\n\\kappa=N-\\sum_{h=3}^n\\frac{\\gamma_h}{a_h}\\geq0.\n\\]\nThe pair exponents with this label lie in \\(\\kappa\\Delta(A,B)\\).</p><p>Take their polynomial span in \\(k[x,y]\\) and replace it by its least exponents in the coordinates \\(\\xi,\\eta\\) and order \\(\\nu_t\\) of Lemma 4.1. Proposition 1 preserves cardinality and gradedness: the row spaces multiply into the row with degree and label added, and the same coordinates and order are used throughout.</p><p>For fixed \\(N\\), choose a basis with distinct least exponents in each row and apply the weight-realization assertion of Proposition 1 to the finite union of these bases. Its common weight vector allows formal jet transfer to apply to all labels at once, giving backward jet transfer. The old points can be chosen in the dense open torus of\n\\[\n\\mathbb A^2\\times T^{n-2}.\n\\]</p><p>Lemma 4.1 places the new pair exponents in \\(\\kappa Q\\). Apply the shear of Lemma 4.2 and compress in the second coordinate. Proposition 2 and Lemma 4.2 give the bound \\(\\kappa P\\). It remains to turn this triangle into \\(\\Delta(w,H/w)\\).</p><p>Complete the primitive vector \\(u\\) to an integral basis \\((u,v)\\) with\n\\[\n\\det(u,v)=1,\n\\]\nand use the coordinates in that basis. This is a unimodular change, and its second coordinate is precisely\n\\[\nz\\longmapsto\\det(u,z).\n\\]\nWrite \\(q_0&lt;q_1&lt;q_2\\) for the three vertex heights of the transformed triangle. Its horizontal slice length is linear from zero at \\(q_0\\) to a maximum at \\(q_1\\), and linear back to zero at \\(q_2\\). The maximum is \\(w\\), since area is preserved and\n\\[\n\\frac H2\n=\n\\frac12(q_2-q_0)(\\text{maximum slice length}),\n\\qquad\nq_2-q_0=R=H/w.\n\\]</p><p>Compressing in the first coordinate therefore gives the bound\n\\[\n\\operatorname{conv}\\{(0,q_0),(w,q_1),(0,q_2)\\}.\n\\]\nIts vertical slice at first coordinate \\(h\\in[0,w]\\) has length\n\\[\nR(1-h/w).\n\\]\nCompression in the second coordinate gives \\(\\Delta(w,R)\\).</p><p>These slice statements commute with multiplication by \\(\\kappa\\), so the resulting pair bound is \\(\\kappa\\Delta(w,R)\\) for every label. If \\(\\kappa=0\\), its only possible pair exponent is \\((0,0)\\), which remains unchanged at every step.</p><p>Restoring the labels gives\n\\[\nS'_N\\subset N\\Delta(w,R,a_3,\\ldots,a_n).\n\\]\nEvery step preserves cardinality and gradedness and has backward jet transfer, by Propositions 1 and 2. Their composition has all the asserted properties.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Lemma 4.2 of the paper:", "statement": "<p>**Lemma 4.2 of the paper: Nodal support bound.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(A,B,H,d,W,c\\) from Proposition 4, and work in \\(\\mathbb A_k^2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(t&gt;0\\), order pairs \\((\\alpha,\\beta)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\geq0}^2\\) by the lexicographic order of \\((\\alpha+t\\beta,\\beta)\\). Denote the least exponent by \\(\\nu_t\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Given \\(t&gt;0\\), put\n  \\[\n  a=\\frac{Wt}{1+t},\\qquad b=\\frac{W}{1+t},\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  Q=\\operatorname{conv}\\{(0,0),(a,0),(c,c),(0,b)\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are formal coordinates \\(\\xi,\\eta\\) at the origin of \\(\\mathbb A_k^2\\) with\n\\[\ny^2+xy-x^3=\\xi\\eta\n\\]\nsuch that, whenever \\(\\kappa\\geq0\\) and \\(0\\neq f\\in k[x,y]\\) has all its exponents in \\(\\kappa\\Delta(A,B)\\), its expansion in these coordinates satisfies\n\\[\n\\nu_t(f)\\in\\kappa Q.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write\n\\[\ng=y^2+xy-x^3.\n\\]\nThe parametrization\n\\[\nx=v(v+1),\\qquad y=v^2(v+1)=vx\n\\]\nidentifies the complement of \\(x=0\\) in \\(g=0\\) with\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Spec}k[v,1/(v(v+1))]:\n\\]\nthe inverse is \\(v=y/x\\). Consequently, a polynomial vanishes under this parametrization if and only if it is divisible by \\(g\\). Indeed, vanishing implies membership in \\((g)\\) after inverting \\(x\\), and \\(g\\) is relatively prime to \\(x\\) in the unique factorization domain \\(k[x,y]\\).</p><p>The polynomial\n\\[\nZ^2+Z-x\n\\]\nhas simple roots \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\) modulo \\(x\\). Hensel lifting gives roots \\(r_1,r_2\\in k[[x]]\\) with these respective constant terms. Set\n\\[\n\\eta=y-xr_1(x),\\qquad \\xi=y-xr_2(x).\n\\]\nTheir linear terms are \\(y\\) and \\(y+x\\), so they are formal coordinates, and \\(\\xi\\eta=g\\). These assertions also hold in characteristic two: the roots \\(0\\) and \\(-1=1\\) remain distinct and the linear terms remain independent.</p><p>Along the parametrization near \\(v=0\\), uniqueness of the lifted root gives\n\\[\nr_1(x(v))=v,\n\\]\nso \\(\\eta=0\\) and \\(\\xi\\) has order one in \\(v\\). Near \\(v=-1\\), one similarly has \\(\\xi=0\\) and \\(\\eta\\) has order one in \\(v+1\\).</p><p>Factor\n\\[\nf=g^ef_0,\\qquad e\\geq0,\\qquad g\\nmid f_0.\n\\]\nGive \\(x,y\\) the positive weights \\(1/A,1/B\\), and write \\(\\deg_{A,B}\\) for the maximum weight of a polynomial’s monomials. This degree is additive on products, because their highest weight forms multiply nontrivially. Moreover,\n\\[\n\\deg_{A,B}(g)=d,\\qquad\n\\deg_{A,B}(f_0)\\leq\\kappa-ed;\n\\]\nhere\n\\[\n1/A+1/B\\leq\\max\\{3/A,2/B\\}=d.\n\\]\nThus \\(ed\\leq\\kappa\\).</p><p>The nonzero polynomial\n\\[\nF_0(v)=f_0\\bigl(v(v+1),v^2(v+1)\\bigr)\n\\]\nhas degree at most \\(W(\\kappa-ed)\\), since\n\\[\n2i+3j\\leq W(i/A+j/B)\n\\]\nfor every monomial \\(x^iy^j\\) of \\(f_0\\). Its expansions at \\(v=0\\) and \\(v=-1\\) are both nonzero, since \\(k[v]\\) injects into each completed local ring. These expansions are exactly the restrictions of \\(f_0\\) to the two formal branches described above.</p><p>Let\n\\[\n\\nu_t(f_0)=(\\alpha,\\beta),\\qquad \\mu=\\alpha+t\\beta.\n\\]\nEvery term of its formal expansion has weight at least \\(\\mu\\). Restricting to \\(\\eta=0\\) therefore gives order at least \\(\\mu\\) on the branch at \\(v=0\\); restricting to \\(\\xi=0\\) gives order at least \\(\\mu/t\\) on the branch at \\(v=-1\\). The nonzero polynomial \\(F_0\\) consequently satisfies\n\\[\n(1+1/t)\\mu\n\\leq\n\\operatorname{ord}_{v=0}(F_0)\n+\\operatorname{ord}_{v=-1}(F_0)\n\\leq\n\\deg(F_0)\n\\leq\nW(\\kappa-ed).\n\\]\nEquivalently,\n\\[\n(\\alpha,\\beta)\\in(\\kappa-ed)\\Delta(a,b).\n\\]</p><p>Because \\(g=\\xi\\eta\\), multiplicativity of least exponents gives\n\\[\n\\nu_t(f)\n=\n(e,e)+\\nu_t(f_0)\n=\ned(c,c)+\\nu_t(f_0)\n\\in\\kappa Q.\n\\]\nThis also covers \\(\\kappa=0\\), when \\(f\\) is constant.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**The shear and slice calculation in Section 4.2, equations (4.3)–(4.4) of the paper.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**The shear and slice calculation in Section 4.2, equations (4.3)–(4.4) of the paper.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(H,W,c,T\\) from Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(t&gt;T\\), and put\n  \\[\n  a=\\frac{Wt}{1+t},\\qquad b=\\frac{W}{1+t},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  Q=\\operatorname{conv}\\{(0,0),(a,0),(c,c),(0,b)\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The polygon \\(Q\\) is a quadrilateral with the displayed vertex order and area \\(H/2\\). Under the shear\n\\[\n(\\alpha,\\beta)\\longmapsto(\\alpha-\\beta,\\beta),\n\\]\nits vertical slice at horizontal coordinate \\(q\\) has length\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nc(1+q/b),&amp;-b\\leq q\\leq0,\\\\{}[2mm]\nc(1-q/a),&amp;0\\leq q\\leq a.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nReplacing every vertical slice by an interval of the same length starting at zero gives\n\\[\nP=\\operatorname{conv}\\{(-b,0),(a,0),(0,c)\\},\n\\]\nalso of area \\(H/2\\).</p><p>Accordingly, a support bounded by \\(\\kappa Q\\), for any \\(\\kappa\\geq0\\), is bounded by \\(\\kappa P\\) after this shear and compression in the second coordinate.</p>", "proof": "<p>One has\n\\[\na+b=W,\\qquad\n\\frac ca+\\frac cb=\\frac{(1+t)^2}{Tt}&gt;1.\n\\]\nThus \\(Q\\) is a quadrilateral with the vertices in the displayed order, and its area is\n\\[\n\\frac{c(a+b)}2=\\frac H2.\n\\]</p><p>The shear sends its vertices to\n\\[\n(0,0),\\qquad(a,0),\\qquad(0,c),\\qquad(-b,b).\n\\]\nIts vertical slice at horizontal coordinate \\(q\\) has length\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nc(1+q/b),&amp;-b\\leq q\\leq0,\\\\{}[2mm]\nc(1-q/a),&amp;0\\leq q\\leq a.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nCompression in the second coordinate therefore places the support in\n\\[\nP=\\operatorname{conv}\\{(-b,0),(a,0),(0,c)\\}.\n\\]\nIts area is again \\(H/2\\). The slice statements commute with multiplication by \\(\\kappa\\), and Proposition 2 supplies the corresponding support bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Lemma 4.3 of the paper:", "statement": "<p>**Lemma 4.3 of the paper: Prescribed primitive height.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(H,d,W,c,T\\) from Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(w&gt;0\\) satisfy \\(dw&lt;1/16\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(t&gt;T\\), define\n  \\[\n  a=\\frac{Wt}{1+t},\\qquad b=\\frac{W}{1+t},\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  P=\\operatorname{conv}\\{(-b,0),(a,0),(0,c)\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist \\(t&gt;T\\) and a primitive vector \\(u=(i,j)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\) such that the three vertex heights\n\\[\nz\\longmapsto\\det(u,z)\n\\]\nof \\(P\\) are distinct and their range has length \\(H/w\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\nR=H/w,\\qquad a_0=\\frac{WT}{1+T}.\n\\]\nAs \\(t\\) ranges over \\((T,\\infty)\\), the value\n\\[\na=\\frac{Wt}{1+t}\n\\]\nranges over \\((a_0,W)\\).</p><p>For \\(u=(i,j)\\), the heights of \\((-b,0),(a,0),(0,c)\\) are\n\\[\njb,\\qquad-ja,\\qquad i/d.\n\\]\nWe seek \\(j&gt;0\\) and \\(i\\) such that\n\\[\nja+i/d=R,\\qquad i/d&gt;jb.\n\\]\nThe first equality turns the second inequality into \\(R-jW&gt;0\\). Thus we first choose \\(jW\\leq R/2\\), and then use the freedom in \\(a\\) to obtain the equality with \\(i\\) coprime to \\(j\\).</p><p>Take \\(j\\) to be the largest power of two at most\n\\[\n\\frac{R}{2W}=\\frac1{2dw}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nj&gt;\\frac1{4dw}&gt;4.\n\\]\nThe open interval\n\\[\n\\bigl(d(R-jW),\\,d(R-ja_0)\\bigr)\n\\]\nhas length\n\\[\ndj(W-a_0)=\\frac{jT}{1+T}&gt;2,\n\\]\nso it contains an odd integer \\(i\\). Since \\(j\\) is a power of two, \\((i,j)\\) is primitive. There is a unique \\(t&gt;T\\) such that\n\\[\na=\\frac{R-i/d}{j}\\in(a_0,W).\n\\]\nThe resulting heights satisfy\n\\[\n-ja&lt;jb&lt;i/d,\n\\]\n\\[\ni/d-jb=R-jW\\geq R/2&gt;0,\n\\qquad\ni/d+ja=R.\n\\]\nThis gives the required height range and distinctness.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Proposition 5.3 of the paper:", "statement": "<p>**Proposition 5.3 of the paper: Jets at the geometric generic tuple.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be an algebraically closed field of positive characteristic.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X/k\\) be a smooth integral projective variety of dimension \\(n\\geq2\\), and let \\(L\\) be ample.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(V=L^n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For each \\(r\\geq1\\), let\n  \\[\n  U_r=\\{(x_1,\\ldots,x_r)\\in X^r:x_i\\neq x_j\\text{ for }i\\neq j\\},\n  \\qquad\n  K_r=\\overline{k(U_r)},\n  \\]\n  and let \\(p_1,\\ldots,p_r\\in X(K_r)\\) be the geometric generic tuple.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an integer \\(r_0=r_0(X,L)\\) such that, for every integer \\(r\\geq r_0\\) and every real number\n\\[\n0&lt;\\theta&lt;\\left(\\frac Vr\\right)^{1/n},\n\\]\nthere is an integer \\(N_0=N_0(r,\\theta)\\) such that, for every \\(N\\geq N_0\\), the sections of \\(NL\\) separate jets through order \\(\\lfloor N\\theta\\rfloor\\) at some ordered \\(r\\)-tuple of distinct \\(k\\)-points, which may depend on \\(N\\).</p><p>For each such \\(N\\), the same jet separation holds after base change to \\(K_r\\) at the geometric generic tuple \\(p_1,\\ldots,p_r\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Take the graded family \\(S_N\\) and simplex\n\\[\nP_0=\\Delta(a_1,\\ldots,a_n)\n\\]\nfrom Proposition 3. Thus\n\\[\n\\prod_j a_j=V,\\qquad\nS_N\\subset NP_0,\\qquad\n\\#S_N=\\frac{V}{n!}N^n+o(N^n).\n\\]\nLet\n\\[\n\\ell=\\min_j a_j,\n\\]\nand choose \\(r_0\\) so large that\n\\[\nw:=\\left(\\frac Vr\\right)^{1/n}&lt;\\frac{\\ell}{100}\n\\qquad(r\\geq r_0).\n\\]</p><p>Fix one such \\(r\\). Reshape the first two intercepts to\n\\[\n(w,a_1a_2/w),\n\\]\nthen reshape the second and third intercepts, and continue through the \\((n-1)\\)-st and \\(n\\)-th intercepts.</p><p>At every step, both intercepts \\(A,B\\) being reshaped are at least \\(\\ell\\): untouched intercepts have this property, and the carried intercept \\(AB/w\\) is larger than \\(A\\) because \\(B&gt;w\\). Consequently\n\\[\nw\\max(3/A,2/B)\\leq\\frac{3w}{\\ell}&lt;\\frac1{16},\n\\]\nas required by Proposition 4.</p><p>The product of all intercepts remains \\(V\\). The resulting graded family \\(F_N\\) therefore satisfies\n\\[\nF_N\\subset NP_*\\cap\\mathbb Z^n,\n\\qquad\nP_*=\\Delta(w,\\ldots,w,rw),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\#F_N\n=\n\\frac{V}{n!}N^n+o(N^n)\n=\n\\operatorname{vol}(P_*)N^n+o(N^n).\n\\]\nHere the last intercept is\n\\[\n\\frac{V}{w^{n-1}}=rw.\n\\]\nThe transformations preserve backward jet transfer, so jet separation by \\(M(F_N)\\) will imply jet separation by \\(H^0(X,NL)\\).</p><p>Fix \\(0&lt;\\theta&lt;w\\). To apply Lemma 5.1, contract \\(P_*\\) and then translate it away from the coordinate faces. Choose \\(\\lambda\\) with\n\\[\n\\theta/w&lt;\\lambda&lt;1,\n\\]\nand choose a vector \\(v\\in\\mathbb R_{&gt;0}^n\\) small enough that\n\\[\nv+\\lambda P_*\\subset\\operatorname{int}(P_*).\n\\]\nFor example, it suffices that\n\\[\n\\sum_{j&lt;n}\\frac{v_j}{w}+\\frac{v_n}{rw}&lt;1-\\lambda.\n\\]</p><p>Choose vectors \\(v_N\\in N^{-1}\\mathbb Z^n\\) tending to \\(v\\). For all sufficiently large \\(N\\), the sets \\(v_N+\\lambda P_*\\) lie in a common compact subset \\(K\\) of \\(\\operatorname{int}(P_*)\\). Applying Lemma 5.1 gives\n\\[\nNv_N+\\bigl(N\\lambda P_*\\cap\\mathbb Z^n\\bigr)\\subset F_N\n\\]\nfor all sufficiently large \\(N\\).</p><p>Put\n\\[\nm=\\lfloor N\\theta\\rfloor.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\theta&lt;\\lambda w\\), one has\n\\[\nm+1\\leq N\\lambda w\n\\]\nfor all sufficiently large \\(N\\). The defining inequality for \\(E_m\\) in Lemma 5.2 therefore gives\n\\[\nE_m\\subset N\\lambda P_*\\cap\\mathbb Z^n.\n\\]</p><p>Choose distinct \\(c_1,\\ldots,c_r\\in k^\\times\\) and apply Lemma 5.2. Multiplication by the Laurent monomial with exponent \\(Nv_N\\in\\mathbb Z^n\\) is multiplication by a unit on the torus, hence induces an automorphism on every jet space. Thus the displayed containment implies that \\(M(F_N)\\) separates jets through order \\(m\\) at these \\(r\\) points.</p><p>Backward jet transfer through all reshapings and Proposition 3 gives the asserted jet separation at an ordered tuple on \\(X\\).</p><p>For each fixed \\(r,\\theta\\), and sufficiently large \\(N\\), the locus in \\(U_r\\) where this evaluation map is surjective is a nonempty open set, by Proposition 1. The variety \\(U_r\\) is integral, so its generic point belongs to that open set. Base change of the jet evaluation map to \\(K_r\\) preserves surjectivity and identifies its targets with the jets at \\(p_1,\\ldots,p_r\\). The same geometric generic tuple therefore satisfies every required jet-separation statement.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Lemma 5.1 of the paper:", "statement": "<p>**Lemma 5.1 of the paper: Interior filling.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This lemma is purely real and combinatorial; no field or variety is assumed.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\), \\(n\\geq1\\), be a compact convex set with nonempty interior.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose finite sets\n  \\[\n  F_N\\subset NP\\cap\\mathbb Z^n,\n  \\qquad N\\geq1,\n  \\]\n  satisfy\n  \\[\n  F_N+F_{N'}\\subset F_{N+N'}\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  \\#F_N=\\operatorname{vol}(P)N^n+o(N^n).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every compact set \\(K\\subset\\operatorname{int}(P)\\), there is an integer \\(N_K\\) such that\n\\[\nNK\\cap\\mathbb Z^n\\subset F_N\n\\qquad(N\\geq N_K).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The boundary of a convex body has measure zero, so lattice-point counting by Riemann sums gives\n\\[\n\\#(NP\\cap\\mathbb Z^n)\n=\n\\operatorname{vol}(P)N^n+o(N^n).\n\\]\nConsequently the number of missing lattice points\n\\[\nb_N:=\\#\\bigl((NP\\cap\\mathbb Z^n)\\setminus F_N\\bigr)\n\\]\nis \\(o(N^n)\\). We show that a point of \\(NK\\) has too many decompositions into two points in nearly equal degrees for all of them to involve a missing point.</p><p>Assume \\(K\\) is nonempty, and choose \\(\\rho&gt;0\\) so that the closed ball of radius \\(\\rho\\) about every point of \\(K\\) lies in \\(P\\). Write\n\\[\nN=N_1+N_2,\\qquad\nN_1=\\lfloor N/2\\rfloor,\\qquad\nN_2=\\lceil N/2\\rceil.\n\\]\nFor \\(y\\in NK\\cap\\mathbb Z^n\\), consider all lattice points\n\\[\ny_1\\in\\mathbb Z^n,\n\\qquad\n\\left\\|y_1-\\frac{N_1}{N}y\\right\\|&lt;\\rho N_1,\n\\qquad\ny_2:=y-y_1.\n\\]\nBoth \\(y_1/N_1\\) and \\(y_2/N_2\\) lie in the radius-\\(\\rho\\) ball about \\(y/N\\). Thus\n\\[\ny_i\\in N_iP\\cap\\mathbb Z^n.\n\\]</p><p>The ball from which \\(y_1\\) is chosen contains at least \\(cN^n\\) lattice points for a constant \\(c&gt;0\\) independent of \\(y\\) and all sufficiently large \\(N\\): an inscribed cube gives this bound uniformly in the center.</p><p>Among these choices, at most \\(b_{N_1}\\) have \\(y_1\\notin F_{N_1}\\), and at most \\(b_{N_2}\\) have \\(y_2\\notin F_{N_2}\\), since \\(y_1\\mapsto y-y_1\\) is injective. As\n\\[\nb_{N_1}+b_{N_2}=o(N^n),\n\\]\nat least one choice has \\(y_i\\in F_{N_i}\\). Gradedness then gives\n\\[\ny=y_1+y_2\\in F_N.\n\\]\nAll bounds are uniform in \\(y\\), which proves the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Lemma 5.2 of the paper:", "statement": "<p>**Lemma 5.2 of the paper: Jets from an elongated simplex.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For this lemma, \\(k\\) may be any field containing distinct nonzero elements \\(c_1,\\ldots,c_r\\); algebraic closedness and positive characteristic are not required.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n,r\\geq1\\) and \\(m\\geq0\\) be integers.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  E_m=\n  \\left\\{\n  (\\beta,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\geq0}^{n-1}\\times\\mathbb Z_{\\geq0}:\n  |\\beta|+\\frac br&lt;m+1\n  \\right\\},\n  \\qquad\n  |\\beta|=\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}\\beta_j.\n  \\]\n  For \\(n=1\\), the tuple \\(\\beta\\) is empty and \\(|\\beta|=0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The monomial space \\(M(E_m)\\) separates jets through order \\(m\\) at the \\(r\\) points\n\\[\nq_i=(1,\\ldots,1,c_i)\\in T^n(k).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write the coordinates as \\((x_1,\\ldots,x_{n-1},Y)\\) and put\n\\[\nx'-1=(x_1-1,\\ldots,x_{n-1}-1).\n\\]\nAn arbitrary jet through order \\(m\\) at \\(q_i\\) has a unique expression\n\\[\n\\sum_{|\\alpha|\\leq m}(x'-1)^\\alpha h_{i,\\alpha}(Y-c_i),\n\\qquad\n\\deg h_{i,\\alpha}\\leq m-|\\alpha|.\n\\]</p><p>For each \\(\\alpha\\), the Chinese remainder theorem gives a polynomial \\(h_\\alpha(Y)\\) satisfying\n\\[\nh_\\alpha(Y)\n\\equiv h_{i,\\alpha}(Y-c_i)\n\\pmod{(Y-c_i)^{m-|\\alpha|+1}}\n\\qquad(1\\leq i\\leq r),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\deg h_\\alpha&lt;r(m-|\\alpha|+1).\n\\]\nIndeed, the ideals in this display are pairwise comaximal, and their product is generated by a monic polynomial of degree \\(r(m-|\\alpha|+1)\\).</p><p>The polynomial\n\\[\n\\sum_{|\\alpha|\\leq m}(x'-1)^\\alpha h_\\alpha(Y)\n\\]\nrealizes the prescribed jets. Each monomial \\(x'^\\beta Y^b\\) in its expansion satisfies \\(\\beta_j\\leq\\alpha_j\\) for every \\(j\\) and\n\\[\nb&lt;r(m-|\\alpha|+1)\n\\]\nfor some \\(\\alpha\\). Hence\n\\[\n|\\beta|+\\frac br&lt;m+1,\n\\]\nso the polynomial belongs to \\(M(E_m)\\).</p><p>This uses powers of local parameters and the Chinese remainder theorem, and therefore works in every characteristic.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Lemma 6.1 of the paper:", "statement": "<p>**Lemma 6.1 of the paper: Jets and curve multiplicities.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Y\\) be a smooth integral projective variety over an algebraically closed field of arbitrary characteristic.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\) be a line bundle on \\(Y\\), and let \\(q_1,\\ldots,q_r\\) be distinct points.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(H^0(Y,A)\\) separates jets through order \\(m\\geq1\\) at these points.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every integral curve \\(C\\subset Y\\) meeting at least one of the marked points satisfies\n\\[\nA\\cdot C\n\\geq\nm\\sum_{i=1}^r\\operatorname{mult}_{q_i}C,\n\\]\nwhere multiplicity is zero at a point outside \\(C\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose such a point \\(q_j\\). Write\n\\[\nR=\\mathcal O_{C,q_j}\n\\]\nand let \\(\\mathfrak n\\) be its maximal ideal. The vector space\n\\[\n\\mathfrak n^m/\\mathfrak n^{m+1}\n\\]\nis nonzero: otherwise Nakayama’s lemma would give \\(\\mathfrak n^m=0\\), contrary to \\(R\\) being a one-dimensional local domain.</p><p>In a local frame of \\(A\\), lift a nonzero class in this vector space to an ambient jet of order \\(m\\) at \\(q_j\\). This lift can be chosen in the image of \\(\\mathfrak m_{Y,q_j}^m\\). Prescribe that jet at \\(q_j\\) and the zero jet at all other marked points. Jet separation produces a section\n\\[\n\\sigma\\in H^0(Y,A)\n\\]\nwhich vanishes to order at least \\(m\\) at every \\(q_i\\) and whose restriction to \\(C\\) is nonzero.</p><p>The local multiplicity estimate used here is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{length}_R(R/(f))\n\\geq\nm\\,e(R)\n\\qquad\n(0\\neq f\\in\\mathfrak n^m),\n\\]\nwhere \\(e(R)\\) is the Hilbert–Samuel multiplicity of a one-dimensional Noetherian local domain \\((R,\\mathfrak n)\\).</p><p>Indeed, for positive integers \\(t\\), the nonzero divisor \\(f\\) gives\n\\[\nt\\,\\operatorname{length}_R(R/(f))\n=\n\\operatorname{length}_R(R/(f^t))\n\\geq\n\\operatorname{length}_R(R/\\mathfrak n^{mt})\n=\nmt\\,e(R)+O(1).\n\\]\nDivide by \\(t\\) and let \\(t\\) tend to infinity.</p><p>Applying this estimate to the local equations of \\(\\sigma|_C\\) at the marked points proves the inequality: the sum of all local zero lengths of a nonzero section of \\(A|_C\\) is\n\\[\n\\deg(A|_C)=A\\cdot C.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Lemma 6.2 of the paper:", "statement": "<p>**Lemma 6.2 of the paper: Volume bound.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Y\\) be a smooth integral projective variety of dimension \\(d\\geq2\\) over an algebraically closed field of arbitrary characteristic.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\) be ample, and let \\(q_1,\\ldots,q_r\\) be distinct points.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One has\n\\[\n\\inf_{\\substack{C\\text{ integral}\\\\\nC\\cap\\{q_1,\\ldots,q_r\\}\\neq\\varnothing}}\n\\frac{A\\cdot C}{\\sum_i\\operatorname{mult}_{q_i}C}\n\\leq\n\\left(\\frac{A^d}{r}\\right)^{1/d}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Denote the infimum by \\(\\varepsilon\\). If \\(\\varepsilon=0\\), the conclusion is immediate. Otherwise, blow up the marked points:\n\\[\n\\pi:\\widetilde Y\\longrightarrow Y,\n\\qquad\nE_1,\\ldots,E_r\\subset\\widetilde Y.\n\\]\nFor each rational number \\(0\\leq s&lt;\\varepsilon\\), the divisor class\n\\[\nD_s=\\pi^*A-s\\sum_iE_i\n\\]\nis nef.</p><p>To check this on curves, first observe that\n\\[\nD_s|_{E_i}=s\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb P^{d-1}}(1),\n\\]\nwhich has nonnegative degree on every curve in \\(E_i\\). Every other integral curve in \\(\\widetilde Y\\) is the strict transform \\(\\widetilde C\\) of an integral curve \\(C\\subset Y\\), and\n\\[\nE_i\\cdot\\widetilde C=\\operatorname{mult}_{q_i}C.\n\\]\nThus \\(D_s\\cdot\\widetilde C\\geq0\\) by the definition of \\(\\varepsilon\\); for curves missing all marked points this follows from the ampleness of \\(A\\).</p><p>Here is the local justification of the displayed equality, including singular curves. If \\(q_i\\in C\\), the Rees algebra construction identifies \\(\\widetilde C\\) with the blow-up of \\(C\\) at the marked points. Indeed, the ambient Rees algebra surjects onto the Rees algebra of the restricted ideal. The latter is integral, and its \\(\\operatorname{Proj}\\) agrees with \\(C\\) away from the centers, so its closed image is exactly the strict transform.</p><p>For\n\\[\nR=\\mathcal O_{C,q_i}\n\\]\nwith maximal ideal \\(\\mathfrak n\\), its exceptional fiber is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Proj}(\\operatorname{gr}_{\\mathfrak n}R).\n\\]\nIt is the effective Cartier divisor cut out on \\(\\widetilde C\\) by \\(E_i\\). Its length is \\(e(R)\\): the Hilbert function\n\\[\n\\dim_{k(q_i)}(\\mathfrak n^t/\\mathfrak n^{t+1})\n\\]\nequals \\(e(R)\\) for all sufficiently large \\(t\\), and its eventual value is the length of this zero-dimensional projective scheme. This proves\n\\[\nE_i\\cdot\\widetilde C=\\operatorname{mult}_{q_i}C.\n\\]\nIf \\(q_i\\notin C\\), both sides vanish.</p><p>A nef class on a projective variety is a limit of ample classes and therefore has nonnegative top self-intersection. The exceptional divisors are disjoint, \\(\\pi^*A\\) restricts trivially to each of them, and\n\\[\nE_i^d=(-1)^{d-1}.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n0\\leq D_s^d=A^d-rs^d.\n\\]</p><p>If\n\\[\n\\varepsilon&gt;\\left(\\frac{A^d}{r}\\right)^{1/d},\n\\]\neven if \\(\\varepsilon=+\\infty\\), there is a rational number \\(s\\) strictly between \\((A^d/r)^{1/d}\\) and \\(\\varepsilon\\). The displayed inequality excludes such an \\(s\\), proving the bound.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 5, "cite"], [1, 7, "cite"], [1, 8, "cite"], [1, 9, "cite"], [1, 13, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [4, 1, "cite"], [5, 9, "cite"], [5, 11, "cite"], [6, 5, "cite"], [7, 5, "cite"], [8, 0, "cite"], [8, 13, "cite"], [9, 0, "cite"], [9, 13, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [11, 9, "cite"], [11, 10, "cite"], [12, 9, "cite"], [13, 0, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [15, 13, "cite"], [16, 0, "cite"], [17, 0, "cite"]]});
